rofit per game?" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360147 1113 #uuid "150d488e-5edb-5db7-b1b9-30f4afcb814f" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360147 1145 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game. Using the given probabilities, let's summarize this in a table.  If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{2}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{2}{5})+2(\\frac{3}{10})+3(\\frac{1}{5})+4(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{4}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{10} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $$2.$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360149 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360150 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360151 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360152 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360153 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1041 "selection" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360149 13194139535668 true] [17592186360148 1110 #uuid "9f8392ae-eeac-59fa-a366-f7a5048b6298" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360149 1034 "$$2$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535668 true] [17592186360149 1111 #uuid "ffb2573a-ec84-5535-9bbc-9656f8114ae7" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360150 1034 "$$3.1$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535668 true] [17592186360150 1111 #uuid "f6c2be46-6ad5-56a6-97a8-d704ac9cc22a" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360151 1034 "$$2.8$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535668 true] [17592186360151 1111 #uuid "c8da36fa-25a1-5145-b817-bace98141658" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360152 1034 "$$2.6$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535668 true] [17592186360152 1111 #uuid "dccd7d9b-03e8-529e-a7e2-9a77a5b9c903" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360153 1034 "$$4.2$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535668 true] [17592186360153 1111 #uuid "623a4a4d-1ada-58b1-98ea-2bdc5783710b" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360154 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360155 13194139535668 true] [17592186360154 1089 "q-276299" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360154 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535668 true] [17592186360154 1100 "The absolute difference between the outcomes of one roll of two unfair six-sided dice follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is the expected value of the absolute difference of the outcomes for one roll of the two dice?\n\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{10}$ | $\\frac{9}{50}$ | $\\frac{4}{25}$ | $\\frac{7}{50}$ | $\\frac{3}{25}$ | $\\frac{1}{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360154 1113 #uuid "a56b720e-3b4e-5311-900b-881982f121d6" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360154 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{3}{10})+1(\\frac{9}{50})+2(\\frac{4}{25})+3(\\frac{7}{50})+4(\\frac{3}{25})+5(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}+\\frac{8}{25}+\\frac{21}{50}+\\frac{12}{25}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{50}+\\frac{16}{50}+\\frac{21}{50}+\\frac{24}{50}+\\frac{25}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9+16+21+24+25}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360156 13194139535668 true] [17592186360155 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360157 13194139535668 true] [17592186360155 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360155 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360155 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360157 13194139535668 true] [17592186360155 1110 #uuid "bea45359-2f05-5073-b36f-da9919804ac1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360156 1034 "2" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360156 1111 #uuid "915e94d9-81f6-5d90-a334-763acbe0c14d" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360157 1034 "\\frac{19}{10}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360157 1111 #uuid "8cc93be3-c718-5a1a-8f4b-27f92628c294" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360159 13194139535668 true] [17592186360158 1089 "q-276305" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535668 true] [17592186360158 1100 "Consider a game where each play costs one dime $(10¢).$ For each play, the game dispenses a single coin to the player. At the start of any game, the game machine always contains an equal number of pennies $(1¢),$ nickels $(5¢),$ dimes $(10¢),$ quarters $(25¢),$ and half-dollars $(50¢).$ The dime inserted by the player is separate from the pool of reward coins. What is the expected profit, in cents, for a player who plays twenty games in a row?\n\n$\\text{E}[p]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360158 1113 #uuid "37750ed7-0b60-5664-a260-97d8b547524e" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360158 1145 "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after twenty games, we need to subtract $10\\cdot 20=200$ cents to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings. For each play, the five coins have an equal chance of being dispensed, so each one has a probability of $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $W$ is the amount of winnings (in cents) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $1$ | $5$ | $10$ | $25$ | $50$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =1\\cdot f(1)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10)+25\\cdot f(25)+50\\cdot f(50) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{5})+5(\\frac{1}{5})+10(\\frac{1}{5})+25(\\frac{1}{5})+50(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{5}{5}+\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{25}{5}+\\frac{50}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+5+10+25+50}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{91}{5} \\\\  & =18.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $20$ games is\n\n$$\n20\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=20\\cdot 18.2=364.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $200$ cents paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in cents) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=364-200=164.\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360160 13194139535668 true] [17592186360159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360161 13194139535668 true] [17592186360159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360159 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360161 13194139535668 true] [17592186360159 1110 #uuid "d53a49a9-a1a6-5409-8b13-9b3950a6a48e" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360160 1034 "364" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360160 1111 #uuid "dde2c9ee-400a-5926-ab4a-4020f40c716b" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360161 1034 "164" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360161 1111 #uuid "5603e177-d7a8-532d-b0fa-c0cb653765cd" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360162 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360163 13194139535668 true] [17592186360162 1089 "q-336633" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360162 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535668 true] [17592186360162 1100 "You have a stack of seven cards labeled $1$ through $7.$ If one card is randomly drawn, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360162 1113 #uuid "65b2d25a-e3b1-50f5-a263-d543d9c0a9ad" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360162 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the label on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S={1,2,3,…,7},$ and the corresponding probabilities are all $\\frac{1}{7}:$\n\n$$\nf(1)=f(2)=…=f(7)=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+…+7\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+…+7(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{2}{7}+…+\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2+3+4+5+6+7}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{28}{7} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360164 13194139535668 true] [17592186360163 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360163 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360163 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360164 13194139535668 true] [17592186360163 1110 #uuid "ab8e61c7-87c6-5473-8e3c-959f181bc058" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360164 1034 "4" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360164 1111 #uuid "fd07959c-d4a4-53c0-a107-da54f9dae7a6" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360165 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360166 13194139535668 true] [17592186360165 1089 "q-337036" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360165 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535668 true] [17592186360165 1100 "The rolls of an unfair six-sided die follow the probability mass function $f(x)$ as shown in the table below. What is the expected value of one roll of the die?\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360165 1113 #uuid "79b79a48-fcac-5891-9033-90d9d6b26779" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360165 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(0.1)+2(0.4)+3(0.15)+4(0.2)+5(0.05)+6(0.1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.8+0.45+0.8+0.25+0.6 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360167 13194139535668 true] [17592186360166 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360166 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360166 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360167 13194139535668 true] [17592186360166 1110 #uuid "985d1219-20ce-546a-8679-1d185f1f22bf" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360167 1034 "3" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360167 1111 #uuid "75e333f6-cc58-5a6b-aab6-0e25cced6cba" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360168 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360169 13194139535668 true] [17592186360168 1089 "q-337198" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360168 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535668 true] [17592186360168 1100 "A quality assurance specialist inspects sealed containers for damaged items. The number of damaged items in each container was recorded and summarized below. If $X$ is the number of damaged items in a container, what is the expected value of $X?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| Number of Containers | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Damaged Items | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360168 1113 #uuid "5e645bbd-46af-5ff7-95ba-9a122c0ace98" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360168 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nWe're given the frequencies of the damaged items, so we can start by computing the corresponding probabilities. The total number of containers is $10+8+6+4+2=30,$ so the corresponding probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0)=f(0) & =\\frac{10}{30} \\\\ P(X=1)=f(1) & =\\frac{8}{30} \\\\ P(X=2)=f(2) & =\\frac{6}{30} \\\\ P(X=3)=f(3) & =\\frac{4}{30} \\\\ P(X=4)=f(4) & =\\frac{2}{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe organize these results in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{10}{30}$ | $\\frac{8}{30}$ | $\\frac{6}{30}$ | $\\frac{4}{30}$ | $\\frac{2}{30}$ |\n\nNow, summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{10}{30})+1(\\frac{8}{30})+2(\\frac{6}{30})+3(\\frac{4}{30})+4(\\frac{2}{30}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{30}+\\frac{12}{30}+\\frac{12}{30}+\\frac{8}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360170 13194139535668 true] [17592186360169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360171 13194139535668 true] [17592186360169 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360169 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360169 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360171 13194139535668 true] [17592186360169 1110 #uuid "19ff3705-1099-5eae-83e6-9e0ee22b0abe" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360170 1034 "2" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360170 1111 #uuid "ab1bd5ac-7758-5477-88b6-19fef5a2536c" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360171 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360171 1111 #uuid "aa7072f9-e8dc-50fb-82d1-8aa236d6ed98" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360173 13194139535668 true] [17592186360172 1089 "q-337202" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535668 true] [17592186360172 1100 "A stack of cards has $6$ cards labeled with two, $6$ cards labeled with five, and $8$ cards labeled with eleven. If one card is randomly drawn from the stack, what is its expected value?\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360172 1113 #uuid "f81979b1-8e29-5e9f-9a58-17567ffb01ea" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360172 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn card. Then the support is $S={2,5,11}.$\n\nIn the stack there are $6$ cards labeled with two, $6$ cards labeled with five, and $8$ cards labeled with eleven (twenty cards in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{6}{20}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(5) & =\\frac{6}{20}=\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ P(11) & =\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+5\\cdot f(5)+11\\cdot f(11) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{10})+5(\\frac{3}{10})+11(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{44}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+15+44}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360174 13194139535668 true] [17592186360173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360173 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360174 13194139535668 true] [17592186360173 1110 #uuid "0c90edc5-a9c5-5f6e-877d-7301356da936" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360174 1034 "6.5" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360174 1111 #uuid "4ef7ad6d-2c2e-555f-a915-96e6d11effbb" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360175 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360176 13194139535668 true] [17592186360175 1089 "q-337207" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360175 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535668 true] [17592186360175 1100 "A bag has $4$ tiles labeled with two, $5$ tiles labeled with seven, and $3$ tiles labeled with fifteen. If one tile is randomly drawn from the bag, what is its expected value? Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\text{E}[X]=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360175 1113 #uuid "10893bf0-830a-5cee-a928-0d250977ec93" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360175 1145 "The expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the value labeled on a randomly drawn tile. Then the support is $S={2,7,15}.$\n\nIn the bag, there are $5$ tiles labeled with seven, $4$ tiles labeled with two, and $3$ tiles labeled with fifteen (twelve tiles in total), so the probabilities are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ P(7) & =\\frac{5}{12} \\\\ P(15) & =\\frac{3}{12}=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =2\\cdot f(2)+7\\cdot f(7)+15\\cdot f(15) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3})+7(\\frac{5}{12})+15(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{35}{12}+\\frac{15}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12}+\\frac{35}{12}+\\frac{45}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{8+35+45}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360176 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360177 13194139535668 true] [17592186360176 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360176 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360176 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360177 13194139535668 true] [17592186360176 1110 #uuid "32a466b7-aed1-5831-98a3-83fb6a8e6208" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360177 1034 "\\frac{22}{3}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360177 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360177 1111 #uuid "af52b892-d559-5f16-9e01-ca1d3a2b011d" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360178 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360179 13194139535668 true] [17592186360178 1089 "q-337337" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360178 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535668 true] [17592186360178 1100 "Suppose we play a raffle that costs $$2$ per day. Each player chooses a four-digit number between $0000$ and $9999,$ inclusive, and one winning number is randomly selected each day. The owner of the chosen number each day wins $$2,000.$ What is the expected daily profit of a player in this game?\n\nThe expected profit is $-$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360178 1113 #uuid "64349391-88b5-5573-bd34-7109d0cd6121" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360178 1145 "Let $p$ represent the daily profit in this game. There are $10,000$ possible numbers that can be selected. One of these numbers is the winning number. In the game, there are two possible outcomes:\n\n- If the player's number is selected, they win $$2,000.$ This means the player’s profit is $$2,000-$2=$1,998,$ so we have $p=1998.$\n\n- If the player's number is not selected, they lose $$2,$ so we have $p=-2.$\n\nThe probabilities for the outcomes are\n\n$$\nP(1998)=\\frac{1}{10,000},\\,P(-2)=1-\\frac{1}{10,000}=\\frac{9,999}{10,000}.\n$$\n\nLet's summarize this in a table. If $p$ is the profit (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(p)$ is the probability mass function for $p,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $p$ | $1998$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(p)$ | $\\frac{1}{10,000}$ | $\\frac{9,999}{10,000}$ |\n\nTherefore, the expected value of $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[p] & =1998\\cdot f(1998)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2) \\\\  & =1998(\\frac{1}{10,000})-2(\\frac{9,999}{10,000}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1998}{10,000}-\\frac{19,998}{10,000} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18,000}{10,000} \\\\  & =-1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-$\\,1.8.$" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360179 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360180 13194139535668 true] [17592186360179 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535668 true] [17592186360179 1041 "field-1" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360179 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360180 13194139535668 true] [17592186360179 1110 #uuid "84d9192a-62de-5e80-9b7b-8af9e974e1f0" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360180 1034 "1.80" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535668 true] [17592186360180 1111 #uuid "97c8dbb2-0dc8-51fa-a38d-d73de388702c" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360182 13194139535668 true] [17592186360181 1089 "q-337341" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535668 true] [17592186360181 1100 "Consider a game of chance with $4$ prizes ($$1$, $$2$, $$3$, and $$5$) where each outcome has a specific probability of happening, shown in the table below. \n\n| $\\text{Number}$ | $\\text{Probability}$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $$1$ | $25%$ |\n| $$2$ | $25%$ |\n| $$3$ | $30%$ |\n| $$5$ | $20%$ |\n\nWhat is the expected profit per game?\n\nThe expected profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360181 1113 #uuid "babca209-29c4-5863-851f-f372bb499e6e" 13194139535668 true] [17592186360181 1145 "Let $p$ represent the profit on a single game, in dollars. 